WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.560 --> 00:00:06.540
SPEAKER 1: I've known Larry Arnn
for a quarter of a century now.

00:00:06.540 --> 00:00:10.110
I was-- I served on the board
of his Claremont Institute out

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:11.420
in California.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:13.930
And about 20 years ago,
he came to me and said,

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:18.840
this Hillsdale College place
has offered me its presidency.

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:24.742
And using my wise counsel,
I said, don't do that.

00:00:24.742 --> 00:00:26.700
I mean, you're running
your own institute here.

00:00:26.700 --> 00:00:27.517
What you say goes.

00:00:27.517 --> 00:00:29.850
And you're going to work with
faculty and administration

00:00:29.850 --> 00:00:31.320
and these wild kids here?

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.700
You know--

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:35.882
Happily, he ignored
my sage advice.

00:00:35.882 --> 00:00:37.590
And it's one of the
best things that ever

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.570
happened to this institution.

00:00:39.570 --> 00:00:41.089
I do think that--

00:00:41.089 --> 00:00:43.903
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:49.890
I do think when the complete
history of Hillsdale

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:53.010
is written one day, I think the
years of Larry Arnn's tenure,

00:00:53.010 --> 00:00:55.680
with the leadership of Bill
Broad back on the board,

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.705
will be looked at as the
golden years of Hillsdale.

00:00:59.705 --> 00:01:01.330
It has been a privilege
to be with him.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.110
He's a great man, in large
part because his partner is

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:05.880
a great woman, the lovely Penny.

00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:09.720
[CHEERING]

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:14.220
And yeah.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.000
He's a great man.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.080
But for Leslie and
me more importantly,

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:18.000
he's a great friend.

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.830
Ladies and gentlemen,
Dr. Larry Arnn.

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:34.880
LARRY ARNN: Good evening.

00:01:34.880 --> 00:01:36.890
I am a partner with Penny.

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:37.940
I'm the junior partner.

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:43.630
Pat is one of my oldest friends.

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:50.470
And he's so faithful
and consistent and calm

00:01:50.470 --> 00:01:53.010
and possessed of good judgment.

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:54.010
And I'm grateful to him.

00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:04.580
I thank Victor and
Mark and Molly,

00:02:04.580 --> 00:02:06.230
who in their each
separate ways are

00:02:06.230 --> 00:02:09.530
forces of nature and among
the most important people

00:02:09.530 --> 00:02:10.150
in the world.

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:23.000
I single out Nancy
Johnson to thank,

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.780
who's the head of
our capital campaign.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.620
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:33.110
Where is she?

00:02:33.110 --> 00:02:34.710
Where's Nancy?

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:36.704
Yeah, stand up.

00:02:36.704 --> 00:02:40.008
[APPLAUSE]

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.730
I came here just
about 20 years ago.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.540
And there were three people
working in my office,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:49.360
one of them Nancy.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:50.830
And two of them,
including Nancy,

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.940
fled in terror
within two months.

00:02:54.940 --> 00:02:58.490
She went to a more sane place,
working for John Salvini.

00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:01.120
But I've never known her
not to do a thing well,

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:04.330
nor for that matter, John
Salvini or Rich [? Payway, ?]

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:06.820
who managed the
construction of the chapel.

00:03:06.820 --> 00:03:15.924
And my colleagues-- you know
what I think, but I love you.

00:03:15.924 --> 00:03:19.333
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:23.472 --> 00:03:25.930
I said that in part because
I've been cranky this afternoon

00:03:25.930 --> 00:03:30.120
because some of
you couldn't hear.

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:32.300
And I made the
point to somebody,

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:33.370
oh, it was so wonderful.

00:03:33.370 --> 00:03:36.340
And I said, you know,
in modern times,

00:03:36.340 --> 00:03:38.560
we will never get a
better opportunity

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:42.110
to explain the meaning
of Hillsdale College

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:44.260
than this morning.

00:03:44.260 --> 00:03:48.860
And about 62% of the people
couldn't hear very well.

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:52.400
And you know, it tells the
college, we're not in prisoner

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:53.240
taking business.

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.650
We-- you know,
we're cocky, right?

00:03:58.650 --> 00:04:00.610
We think we're the best.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.100
And we think that's
not good enough.

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.840
So I apologize to you.

00:04:04.840 --> 00:04:06.660
And if you want to hear--

00:04:06.660 --> 00:04:09.510
I also noticed a funny
thing, and that is, it

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:11.407
was the people who
sat in the places

00:04:11.407 --> 00:04:12.990
where they couldn't
hear very well who

00:04:12.990 --> 00:04:15.757
were the most complimentary
about what I had to say.

00:04:15.757 --> 00:04:19.166
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:24.938 --> 00:04:25.980
What do you make of that?

00:04:30.480 --> 00:04:32.730
But you can get a tape of
that if you want to see it.

00:04:32.730 --> 00:04:34.560
And Justice Thomas was there.

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:36.560
And the choir was gorgeous.

00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:38.840
And the building is,
you know, not bad.

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:42.744
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:50.160
So the last time I talked about
the past, what the college is

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.700
for, and then tonight,
I'm going to talk not too

00:04:53.700 --> 00:04:54.810
long about the future.

00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:01.840
It's easy to predict
the future in one way.

00:05:01.840 --> 00:05:03.650
The college stands
for some things

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:06.410
that it claims are eternal.

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:09.720
Let's just say as
long as nature lasts.

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:12.930
So is there a time
conceivable when we won't

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:15.690
need to have a good character?

00:05:15.690 --> 00:05:17.860
What is a good character?

00:05:17.860 --> 00:05:22.890
It's-- we're this strange
creature is, right?

00:05:22.890 --> 00:05:25.060
We have these bodies.

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.910
They have these needs.

00:05:26.910 --> 00:05:28.500
They hurt.

00:05:28.500 --> 00:05:30.660
And they feel pleasure.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:36.670
And the first of primary virtues
concerned dealing with those.

00:05:36.670 --> 00:05:39.056
Do you want to be a coward?

00:05:39.056 --> 00:05:41.125
Do you want to be a dissolute?

00:05:41.125 --> 00:05:44.850
Do you think you can mount
to anything at all if you're

00:05:44.850 --> 00:05:46.590
either of those things?

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:48.420
Or any of the
other moral virtues

00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:51.490
which lead up toward justice?

00:05:51.490 --> 00:05:54.090
So as long as there
are people, there

00:05:54.090 --> 00:05:56.220
will need to be character.

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.340
And all of these things, by
the way, I'm going to comment--

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:03.360
it's shocking that we
don't teach these things,

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:05.758
because they are, in fact,
the most obvious things

00:06:05.758 --> 00:06:06.300
in the world.

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:14.120
Is there going to be a time
when we don't need to learn--

00:06:14.120 --> 00:06:15.570
what is the thing about us?

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.970
We're the only ones, right?

00:06:17.970 --> 00:06:21.290
I have a favorite
translator, although he's

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:24.140
idiosyncratic in the
way he translates.

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.600
The first sentence of
Aristotle's Metaphysics

00:06:26.600 --> 00:06:33.990
is, "The human soul
stretches itself out to no."

00:06:33.990 --> 00:06:37.230
Don't you see that in
yourselves constantly?

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:40.650
Don't you every day meet
something that Aristotle always

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.340
calls an impasse and aporia.

00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:49.320
That is, you see two things and
they don't make sense together.

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:52.560
And that is an
opportunity for thinking.

00:06:52.560 --> 00:06:55.020
And it's how we
figure out the world.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:57.300
And we love to do that.

00:06:57.300 --> 00:07:02.430
Indeed, much of our sense
of our value of ourselves

00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:05.120
is that we can make
some sense of the world.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:07.910
Learning.

00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.575
Always.

00:07:13.575 --> 00:07:14.075
Freedom.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:20.600
If there's a
choosing being, if--

00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:25.730
we have-- most of you saw
my dogs, our dogs, right?

00:07:25.730 --> 00:07:28.400
Actually there are-- if
there's reincarnation,

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:34.170
and there isn't, then
you want to come back

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:35.450
as one of my wife's dogs.

00:07:40.250 --> 00:07:42.860
And the most troublesome
one is my dog,

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:45.322
but she takes care of it.

00:07:45.322 --> 00:07:46.655
It's the nature of our marriage.

00:07:49.250 --> 00:07:52.070
And they don't choose.

00:07:52.070 --> 00:07:56.180
Now, they select or
opt all the time.

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:59.120
They know when to beg,
and I indulge them.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.670
But you know, we--

00:08:01.670 --> 00:08:04.160
here's the thing about us.

00:08:04.160 --> 00:08:09.500
We can do a thing and
commit ourselves to action

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:15.110
and then wonder for days or
years, was it the right thing?

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:20.480
Something stands outside our
actual actions and judges

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:22.980
whether they were right or not.

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:30.170
And given the capacity for
that, then, it's nature.

00:08:30.170 --> 00:08:32.870
We ought to get to exercise it.

00:08:32.870 --> 00:08:34.760
We need freedom.

00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:36.620
And of course, because
we're made to talk

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:39.830
and therefore we're made
to live together under law,

00:08:39.830 --> 00:08:42.110
we need laws that
protect our freedom.

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:44.960
And as long as there
are people, we're

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:46.140
going to need those things.

00:08:54.710 --> 00:08:56.840
What do you want to know?

00:08:56.840 --> 00:09:00.282
More than anything, what
do you want to know?

00:09:00.282 --> 00:09:01.240
You have these moments.

00:09:01.240 --> 00:09:02.240
I talked about it today.

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:03.452
But none of you heard it.

00:09:03.452 --> 00:09:06.284
[LAUGHTER]

00:09:07.700 --> 00:09:10.700
Don't you have these
moments when you're

00:09:10.700 --> 00:09:14.090
taken quite away
from yourself, even

00:09:14.090 --> 00:09:17.030
in the middle of urgent
things that are bothering you

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:19.580
or are compelling you?

00:09:19.580 --> 00:09:24.260
And you stop and you go,
something occurs to you, right?

00:09:24.260 --> 00:09:29.740
Something high, something
that calls, like the chapel.

00:09:32.910 --> 00:09:37.650
You see things and you think,
some are better than others.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.070
What does it mean, better?

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:46.700
Can't it only be judged in
relation to something perfect?

00:09:46.700 --> 00:09:49.970
Don't you want to complete
the story when you start

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:51.350
making a hierarchy of things?

00:09:51.350 --> 00:09:53.730
Which we can't stop doing.

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:56.400
I happen to be a boy.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:09:58.400
And so, we boys--

00:09:58.400 --> 00:10:03.260
girls are better civilized
than we are, in my experience.

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:07.730
They-- we boys, we like, if
they're sports teams, right,

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:10.300
we want to know,
like, who's better--

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:18.320
the 1927 Yankees or the
1962 Green Bay Packers?

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:21.350
They will never meet,
of course, you know.

00:10:21.350 --> 00:10:23.460
But we just love
things like that.

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:23.960
Why?

00:10:23.960 --> 00:10:29.020
Because we want to
know what the best is.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.140
And when we rank things,
we find things in them

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.930
that seem more perfect
than other things.

00:10:36.930 --> 00:10:42.320
And that means we're
always trying to go up.

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.180
Those are the purposes
of Hillsdale College.

00:10:44.180 --> 00:10:48.110
And the claim is
that they're eternal.

00:10:48.110 --> 00:10:51.260
Meaning, at least as long
as it is their nature.

00:10:53.970 --> 00:10:56.450
So we know if they're right.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:11:01.220
By the way, if they're
wrong, these 175 years

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:03.380
mean nothing whatsoever.

00:11:03.380 --> 00:11:06.620
If they're not true, every
one of them today, then they

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:08.843
were never true.

00:11:08.843 --> 00:11:10.760
If the Declaration of
Independence-- you know,

00:11:10.760 --> 00:11:13.280
John Dewey and Woodrow
Wilson and those guys,

00:11:13.280 --> 00:11:16.840
they're always writing that
it's somewhat outmoded, right?

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.920
But they say, really
good for its time.

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:21.650
And that's an
impossible argument,

00:11:21.650 --> 00:11:26.690
because what it claims is that
it's good for beyond time.

00:11:26.690 --> 00:11:30.550
And that means if it isn't
true now, it wasn't true then.

00:11:30.550 --> 00:11:35.090
And if it was true then,
then it must be true now.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:38.390
And so, the college
actually exists

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:41.630
to invite us to
face those facts.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:43.505
And that's a term
that can't change.

00:11:47.510 --> 00:11:50.010
What can change?

00:11:50.010 --> 00:11:50.760
Everything else.

00:11:55.870 --> 00:12:00.440
Do you think that
tyranny might be coming?

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:01.450
I do.

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:06.740
I don't-- I'm not a
conspiracy theorist.

00:12:06.740 --> 00:12:08.450
I think it's open.

00:12:08.450 --> 00:12:10.280
Look at it, right?

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:12.080
What it-- where is
it evident, right?

00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:20.260
Justice Thomas was
arrested by my claim that--

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:24.000
and Justice Thomas, I can just
tell you something about him.

00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:25.890
He's a student.

00:12:25.890 --> 00:12:28.560
He's just-- you
know, in colleges--

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.708
see, late in my life, I
was 45 years old or so,

00:12:32.708 --> 00:12:34.000
I started working in a college.

00:12:34.000 --> 00:12:37.030
I had avoided it like
the plague before.

00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:39.660
And this happens to
be a good one, right?

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:42.420
So I work here and like it.

00:12:42.420 --> 00:12:44.160
But what do you learn?

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:48.620
You learn that it's
in the student.

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:51.330
And people who are
students by nature,

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:52.650
they get better all the time.

00:12:52.650 --> 00:12:54.870
Justice Thomas is like that.

00:12:54.870 --> 00:12:57.380
And that means you never
say a thing around him

00:12:57.380 --> 00:13:01.060
that he doesn't note it down
and ask you about it later.

00:13:01.060 --> 00:13:02.550
And so, today,
he's asking me who

00:13:02.550 --> 00:13:06.990
is the student of Hegel with
whom John C. Calhoun studied

00:13:06.990 --> 00:13:08.730
at Yale College?

00:13:08.730 --> 00:13:11.700
Because I think-- and I'm not
speaking for him and I would

00:13:11.700 --> 00:13:14.040
not do that, because he's
the greatest man I know,

00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:17.646
and greater than I--

00:13:17.646 --> 00:13:21.950
I think it might mean that
he's connecting things

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:25.470
that friends of mine have
connected for some time now.

00:13:25.470 --> 00:13:30.830
And that is, this here
right-wing Confederacy

00:13:30.830 --> 00:13:35.970
and this here modern
left-wing progressivism,

00:13:35.970 --> 00:13:37.140
they might be akin.

00:13:39.760 --> 00:13:46.180
Or as Churchill put the point,
the Nazis and the Communists,

00:13:46.180 --> 00:13:49.120
they do differ just
exactly as the North

00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:52.480
Pole differs from the South.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:53.700
Look at them.

00:13:53.700 --> 00:13:55.930
They're the same.

00:13:55.930 --> 00:13:58.560
Well, he wanted, so I have to go
look all that up and prove it.

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:01.670
And you know, if I
don't, I'll be shamed.

00:14:01.670 --> 00:14:04.923
And-- but I can.

00:14:04.923 --> 00:14:07.090
Because, you know, just in
the last couple of years,

00:14:07.090 --> 00:14:08.163
I verified it.

00:14:08.163 --> 00:14:10.580
Because I heard it years ago
and I started saying it again

00:14:10.580 --> 00:14:11.705
and everybody said, really?

00:14:16.750 --> 00:14:19.360
That's what that's about, see?

00:14:19.360 --> 00:14:23.230
And I can tell you in simple
terms what it's about.

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:25.720
We live in the age
of modern science,

00:14:25.720 --> 00:14:28.990
and modern science is
different from ancient science.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:32.010
Science is the Latin word
that means "to know".

00:14:32.010 --> 00:14:36.370
Ancient science is
just seeing, to behold,

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.770
to love the things
that are higher.

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:42.260
To do it systematically,
true enough.

00:14:42.260 --> 00:14:44.750
But if your idea is
rather, I'm going

00:14:44.750 --> 00:14:49.420
to study it and test and
show how to change everything

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:51.150
the way I place.

00:14:51.150 --> 00:14:54.820
Bacon says, "torturing nature".

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:58.560
That was born even
before German philosophy.

00:14:58.560 --> 00:15:00.685
But in the 19th century,
these German philosophers,

00:15:00.685 --> 00:15:04.270
right, they came to
understand what they regarded

00:15:04.270 --> 00:15:06.130
as a great opportunity.

00:15:06.130 --> 00:15:09.470
And philosophers are very
wise people, more than we are.

00:15:09.470 --> 00:15:14.117
And so its-- one's reluctant
to talk about their errors.

00:15:14.117 --> 00:15:15.700
And yet there's no
choice, because you

00:15:15.700 --> 00:15:16.867
have to choose between them.

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:21.150
They thought-- they saw
things that they thought

00:15:21.150 --> 00:15:24.270
might be disastrous, but
really, mostly was very hopeful,

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:28.530
and that is,
everything is change.

00:15:28.530 --> 00:15:31.015
And we are all victims
and shaped by that change.

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.770
But now we know that.

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:38.320
We're the first
ones to know that.

00:15:38.320 --> 00:15:41.040
And that changes everything.

00:15:41.040 --> 00:15:47.320
Now we could become the masters
of the process of change.

00:15:50.420 --> 00:15:53.330
We could become
our own creators.

00:15:53.330 --> 00:15:58.130
And then at last,
we would be free.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:00.860
Now, the definition of
freedom I gave earlier

00:16:00.860 --> 00:16:05.760
is, God gave us volition.

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:07.700
The implication is,
we should use it.

00:16:07.700 --> 00:16:08.960
And that's very simple.

00:16:08.960 --> 00:16:11.090
That's an argument
in Aristotle, right?

00:16:11.090 --> 00:16:15.710
Do you ever see
sled dogs running?

00:16:15.710 --> 00:16:18.530
Isn't it a delightful sight?

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.170
And they're made
for that, right?

00:16:20.170 --> 00:16:23.040
Now, imagine horses doing it.

00:16:23.040 --> 00:16:24.660
They wouldn't be
very good at that.

00:16:24.660 --> 00:16:26.990
They're too long legged.

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:27.830
Right?

00:16:27.830 --> 00:16:30.710
And so, they're made for that.

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:33.110
And Aristotle says, well,
you can see that and see

00:16:33.110 --> 00:16:34.280
they're made for that.

00:16:34.280 --> 00:16:38.587
Can you see what a
human being is made for?

00:16:38.587 --> 00:16:40.170
And we all know what
they're made for.

00:16:40.170 --> 00:16:41.280
We see excellent ones.

00:16:41.280 --> 00:16:42.720
And we all admire them.

00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:44.520
Almost all the same, by the way.

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.250
I mean, leaving out of
account Bernie Sanders.

00:16:47.250 --> 00:16:50.687
[LAUGHTER]

00:16:53.640 --> 00:16:57.420
And see this idea that
we can remake everything,

00:16:57.420 --> 00:16:59.760
because now we know
everything is change,

00:16:59.760 --> 00:17:01.980
and we are dominated
by the influences that

00:17:01.980 --> 00:17:03.240
come through change.

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:07.258
What if we could control
all those influences?

00:17:07.258 --> 00:17:09.300
Do you begin to see why
the government would like

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:11.609
to control every single thing?

00:17:11.609 --> 00:17:16.560
Why it's of concern to them what
we say in this room tonight.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:19.680
Why they're not
impressed with the fact

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.599
that, fools though we are,
we have the right to say it.

00:17:24.599 --> 00:17:26.310
They're thinking,
they can't say that.

00:17:26.310 --> 00:17:28.710
They can't say that.

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.090
We will stop that.

00:17:34.430 --> 00:17:36.340
That's the temper that grows up.

00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:40.630
And it came into America, as
it came into Germany before,

00:17:40.630 --> 00:17:44.500
through some very elite
thinkers, the best.

00:17:44.500 --> 00:17:45.340
Right?

00:17:45.340 --> 00:17:49.210
And it came to an America
and it spread, because it's

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:51.550
a very powerful argument.

00:17:51.550 --> 00:17:54.100
It's easier to say
today because we live

00:17:54.100 --> 00:17:55.780
amidst the bureaucratic age.

00:17:55.780 --> 00:17:59.020
And we can see that that's
dangerous and arbitrary.

00:17:59.020 --> 00:18:00.790
Or we here can.

00:18:00.790 --> 00:18:03.700
Many people can't see that.

00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:07.180
But back then, it
was pure hope, right?

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:12.550
Because we could set
everything right.

00:18:12.550 --> 00:18:14.290
And even those great
ones, they didn't

00:18:14.290 --> 00:18:19.260
think enough about the
meaning of that term "right."

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:23.310
Because if everything is
change, by what standard do

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:24.600
you know if a thinker's right?

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:30.160
And what's happened in the
country since that was born?

00:18:30.160 --> 00:18:34.600
First of all, it has
known nothing but progress

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:37.180
anywhere in the world.

00:18:37.180 --> 00:18:39.790
Reagan interrupted
it a little bit

00:18:39.790 --> 00:18:42.760
in this country for a while.

00:18:42.760 --> 00:18:45.700
Trump is fighting against it.

00:18:45.700 --> 00:18:47.382
You know, maybe
artfully, maybe not.

00:18:47.382 --> 00:18:49.340
You know, it depends what
you think about that.

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.980
But there can't really be much
doubt that he's fighting it.

00:18:52.980 --> 00:18:55.960
Oh, I have an email today
from the regulatory czar.

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:01.220
I get my updates every day now.

00:19:01.220 --> 00:19:05.300
And he says, next Wednesday,
something's going to happen.

00:19:05.300 --> 00:19:07.810
Could I be in Washington?

00:19:07.810 --> 00:19:09.020
So you guys are all here.

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:10.210
I don't know how to
think about that.

00:19:10.210 --> 00:19:11.752
But tomorrow, I'm
going to figure out

00:19:11.752 --> 00:19:13.437
if I can go to
Washington on Wednesday.

00:19:13.437 --> 00:19:15.270
And I've got no idea
what's going to happen.

00:19:15.270 --> 00:19:18.262
But whatever it is,
I want to see it.

00:19:18.262 --> 00:19:19.345
You should watch the news.

00:19:22.360 --> 00:19:24.490
Remember, a little
nerdy guy, Paul Ray.

00:19:28.240 --> 00:19:31.930
This has never been set back.

00:19:31.930 --> 00:19:34.150
What's it done?

00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:36.010
Just think of one thing.

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:39.430
It is the most important
thing for the long term,

00:19:39.430 --> 00:19:41.960
although it's not the most
important thing on any given

00:19:41.960 --> 00:19:42.460
day.

00:19:42.460 --> 00:19:43.600
Think of education.

00:19:43.600 --> 00:19:45.880
You know how that's been remade?

00:19:45.880 --> 00:19:48.500
I mean, it is
complete and thorough.

00:19:48.500 --> 00:19:52.780
So you know, John Dewey at
Columbia and Woodrow Wilson

00:19:52.780 --> 00:19:55.750
at Princeton and a few
other places like that,

00:19:55.750 --> 00:19:57.940
they start going from
place to place to place,

00:19:57.940 --> 00:20:02.860
and their apostleship,
their gospel,

00:20:02.860 --> 00:20:05.890
is, we can set everything right.

00:20:05.890 --> 00:20:08.290
Nothing should be in our way.

00:20:08.290 --> 00:20:10.310
We can make it all fine.

00:20:10.310 --> 00:20:12.130
Right?

00:20:12.130 --> 00:20:15.490
Never mind what the
word fine might be.

00:20:15.490 --> 00:20:19.370
Don't want to-- don't want to
spend too much time on that.

00:20:19.370 --> 00:20:22.840
And then, the next thing you
know, it's spread everywhere.

00:20:22.840 --> 00:20:27.520
And it remakes the face
of the modern university.

00:20:27.520 --> 00:20:31.890
You know, there used
to not be departments.

00:20:31.890 --> 00:20:34.550
I see, because he's so tall,
I see [? Caltop ?] over there,

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:35.050
right?

00:20:35.050 --> 00:20:36.970
And I saw Whalen
somewhere before.

00:20:36.970 --> 00:20:38.398
How many years
have we been taught

00:20:38.398 --> 00:20:39.940
been talking about
how we can get rid

00:20:39.940 --> 00:20:42.540
of all the departments
at Hillsdale College?

00:20:42.540 --> 00:20:44.050
And we haven't got
rid of them yet.

00:20:44.050 --> 00:20:46.360
But the core is
the big deal now.

00:20:46.360 --> 00:20:47.170
See?

00:20:47.170 --> 00:20:47.830
Because why?

00:20:47.830 --> 00:20:51.970
The departments are places
of specialized knowledge

00:20:51.970 --> 00:20:53.740
for the remaking of society.

00:20:53.740 --> 00:20:57.220
And that's what John Dewey
analysis in the document that

00:20:57.220 --> 00:21:00.740
founded the Teachers
Union for Colleges,

00:21:00.740 --> 00:21:02.990
on whose blacklist
we are honored to be.

00:21:08.660 --> 00:21:14.680
And that means that there isn't
any common sense way to think

00:21:14.680 --> 00:21:16.030
about what's right and wrong.

00:21:16.030 --> 00:21:18.800
There's only testing.

00:21:18.800 --> 00:21:21.800
And so one of the things
that, when the department lies

00:21:21.800 --> 00:21:24.170
to the university, one of
the things they founded

00:21:24.170 --> 00:21:27.990
was the education department.

00:21:27.990 --> 00:21:31.640
And if you think about that
for a minute, isn't that odd?

00:21:31.640 --> 00:21:34.020
That an institution
of higher education,

00:21:34.020 --> 00:21:38.300
there would be one
department called Education?

00:21:38.300 --> 00:21:41.220
It's actually kind of stupid.

00:21:41.220 --> 00:21:42.890
But what do they teach?

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:43.598
Right?

00:21:43.598 --> 00:21:45.140
We have an education
department here,

00:21:45.140 --> 00:21:46.782
but we don't have
an education major.

00:21:46.782 --> 00:21:48.740
And the reason is, the
people in our department

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:49.970
are really smart.

00:21:49.970 --> 00:21:51.280
Is Copeland here tonight?

00:21:51.280 --> 00:21:53.718
He's been hanging around a lot.

00:21:53.718 --> 00:21:54.260
I don't know.

00:21:56.890 --> 00:21:58.390
He is the chairman.

00:21:58.390 --> 00:21:59.540
Dan Copeland.

00:21:59.540 --> 00:22:07.250
Anyway, they don't
study stuff anymore.

00:22:07.250 --> 00:22:10.700
They study how to deliver stuff.

00:22:10.700 --> 00:22:13.610
Understanding that the
stuff that's delivered

00:22:13.610 --> 00:22:18.630
will be defined by
some central place.

00:22:18.630 --> 00:22:22.950
And so teachers are
now delivery boys.

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:27.630
You know, my father, a high
school teacher in Arkansas--

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:30.760
Hillary Clinton, you know,
because there's a pattern,

00:22:30.760 --> 00:22:31.260
right?

00:22:31.260 --> 00:22:32.968
Bill gets elected governor.

00:22:32.968 --> 00:22:34.260
The biggest thing is education.

00:22:34.260 --> 00:22:36.000
She gets control of that.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.440
Same thing with health
care in America.

00:22:38.440 --> 00:22:41.700
And he just railed about that.

00:22:41.700 --> 00:22:43.470
What are they doing?

00:22:43.470 --> 00:22:47.650
Our school, where I've
worked all my life,

00:22:47.650 --> 00:22:49.650
they think they can run
it better in Little Rock

00:22:49.650 --> 00:22:51.630
than we can run it here?

00:22:51.630 --> 00:22:54.340
And they never
meet the students.

00:22:54.340 --> 00:22:54.840
Right?

00:22:54.840 --> 00:22:56.640
I used to hear that from him.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:22:59.820
And I would say, oh, dad,
you're just annoyed at them

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:01.510
because they're the local thing.

00:23:01.510 --> 00:23:03.348
And they're typical
of the bad things.

00:23:03.348 --> 00:23:04.890
He said, no, they're
worse than that.

00:23:04.890 --> 00:23:07.110
And you know, my
life is a process

00:23:07.110 --> 00:23:09.390
of understanding
my dad was right?

00:23:09.390 --> 00:23:12.840
He was right about that, too.

00:23:12.840 --> 00:23:14.400
So now, what is it?

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:17.590
Now-- I think it
was my daughter,

00:23:17.590 --> 00:23:19.680
Alice, one of the
daughters said to me

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:25.110
that they were watching a
movie with some friends,

00:23:25.110 --> 00:23:28.710
and that some of the people were
not such good friends anyway.

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:31.050
There was a dinner
and the women got up

00:23:31.050 --> 00:23:34.830
and retired to another room.

00:23:34.830 --> 00:23:38.400
And some of the girls
thought that they

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:42.120
had rooms where they
were incarcerated,

00:23:42.120 --> 00:23:45.570
except for the
men left them out.

00:23:45.570 --> 00:23:48.300
That's what
Victorian times were.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:49.950
And I said, goodness
gracious, did they

00:23:49.950 --> 00:23:52.980
never read Jane Austen?

00:23:52.980 --> 00:23:54.450
It wasn't like that.

00:23:54.450 --> 00:23:57.320
Well, they think
it was like that.

00:23:57.320 --> 00:23:58.897
And they think that,
you know, do you

00:23:58.897 --> 00:24:01.230
know the New York Times thing
that's going on right now?

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:02.880
69 project.

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:04.170
Is that what it is?

00:24:04.170 --> 00:24:08.190
America was founded on the
day that the first slave

00:24:08.190 --> 00:24:10.970
ship arrived.

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:12.200
OK, fine.

00:24:12.200 --> 00:24:14.840
But did it arrived in America?

00:24:14.840 --> 00:24:16.790
Because it must
have been earlier.

00:24:16.790 --> 00:24:18.640
Except actually,
it was much later.

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:20.930
With a document that says
all men are created equal.

00:24:23.440 --> 00:24:28.090
So they control what we know.

00:24:28.090 --> 00:24:29.310
And that's comprehensive.

00:24:29.310 --> 00:24:31.530
And it's everything else, too.

00:24:31.530 --> 00:24:33.690
You never know when
you'll hear from them.

00:24:33.690 --> 00:24:37.050
And you never know
what they'll say.

00:24:40.220 --> 00:24:43.520
This kind of government has
to be hostile to anything

00:24:43.520 --> 00:24:44.741
that's independent.

00:24:47.570 --> 00:24:51.500
My son-in-law, Dan,
sitting over there.

00:24:51.500 --> 00:24:52.540
Dan, stand up.

00:24:59.880 --> 00:25:00.810
Where's my daughter?

00:25:00.810 --> 00:25:02.740
Because she's off
somewhere else.

00:25:02.740 --> 00:25:04.170
Yeah, yeah.

00:25:04.170 --> 00:25:05.310
And you saw her today.

00:25:05.310 --> 00:25:07.127
You may wonder why
she married Dan.

00:25:07.127 --> 00:25:07.710
We don't know.

00:25:10.600 --> 00:25:14.520
Anyway, I was making the
argument, and you know,

00:25:14.520 --> 00:25:17.820
did you know that Winston
Churchill is a very great man?

00:25:17.820 --> 00:25:20.460
I mean, really?

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:21.870
I'll be working on Churchill.

00:25:21.870 --> 00:25:23.580
I still do all the time.

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:26.340
And we'll be in bed,
I'll be with opinion,

00:25:26.340 --> 00:25:29.700
we'll take our computers to bed.

00:25:29.700 --> 00:25:32.280
It's not an entirely
unromantic thing.

00:25:32.280 --> 00:25:33.240
We have four children.

00:25:36.955 --> 00:25:37.830
I'll be working away.

00:25:37.830 --> 00:25:41.220
And I'll say to myself, Winston
Churchill was a very great man.

00:25:41.220 --> 00:25:43.800
And she'll say, you've
said that, dear.

00:25:49.250 --> 00:25:51.560
It's from him and
from Aristotle,

00:25:51.560 --> 00:25:55.910
but first from Churchill, I
learned that statesmanship

00:25:55.910 --> 00:26:01.100
is presented as the
guardianship of wisdom,

00:26:01.100 --> 00:26:05.630
because, you know,
people who are wise,

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:07.310
they get that way
because they don't

00:26:07.310 --> 00:26:11.330
go about urgent
politics every day.

00:26:11.330 --> 00:26:15.940
And yet, they're not
necessarily practical people.

00:26:15.940 --> 00:26:19.780
And somebody is
going to oppress them

00:26:19.780 --> 00:26:22.910
unless somebody protects them.

00:26:22.910 --> 00:26:26.190
Here's Winston Churchill
talking about Hitler,

00:26:26.190 --> 00:26:27.320
about who his enemies are.

00:26:30.180 --> 00:26:35.130
Venerable pastures,
upright magistrates,

00:26:35.130 --> 00:26:39.030
world class scientists
and philosophers,

00:26:39.030 --> 00:26:42.570
independent-minded
manly citizens,

00:26:42.570 --> 00:26:46.860
frail, poor old women
of unfashionable opinion

00:26:46.860 --> 00:26:52.530
are invaded, bullied,
and brutalized

00:26:52.530 --> 00:26:57.030
by gangs of armed hooligans.

00:26:57.030 --> 00:26:59.950
Why would they do that?

00:26:59.950 --> 00:27:05.970
The answer is, this passion
for controlling everything

00:27:05.970 --> 00:27:10.140
will brook nothing independent.

00:27:10.140 --> 00:27:14.200
It thinks, it claims, that
it can perfect everything.

00:27:14.200 --> 00:27:18.880
If only it can get the
power to control everything.

00:27:18.880 --> 00:27:22.640
And since the scientific
experiment is the methodology,

00:27:22.640 --> 00:27:25.405
it means nothing can be
outside that experiment.

00:27:28.200 --> 00:27:30.730
I teach a course here, and
we read totalitarian novels.

00:27:34.050 --> 00:27:35.830
There's about seven
of them that we read.

00:27:35.830 --> 00:27:36.880
Various times, right?

00:27:36.880 --> 00:27:41.830
But if you read Darkness
at Noon and 1984,

00:27:41.830 --> 00:27:43.480
you see the spirit of it.

00:27:43.480 --> 00:27:46.120
And most of the totalitarian
novels, not all,

00:27:46.120 --> 00:27:48.730
are written by people
who saw this firsthand.

00:27:48.730 --> 00:27:51.730
Indeed, we're complicit in it.

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:57.250
George Orwell waited in a
cell for three nights in Spain

00:27:57.250 --> 00:28:00.480
for them to come get
him and shoot him.

00:28:00.480 --> 00:28:03.970
And he was a communist
revolutionary

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:06.990
in the Spanish Civil War.

00:28:06.990 --> 00:28:08.970
Arthur Koestler
was an important--

00:28:08.970 --> 00:28:10.500
Darkness at Noon, he wrote.

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:13.980
Arthur Koestler was an important
functionary of the Communist

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:15.970
Party in Hungary.

00:28:15.970 --> 00:28:19.562
And he, too, woke up
in fear of his life.

00:28:19.562 --> 00:28:21.270
And they thought--
that set him thinking,

00:28:21.270 --> 00:28:23.385
you know, really,
what have I done?

00:28:26.990 --> 00:28:28.450
It's horrific.

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:31.340
And it's driven,
made more horrific,

00:28:31.340 --> 00:28:34.700
by the fact that it's driven
by something good, which

00:28:34.700 --> 00:28:41.480
is the wish to ameliorate the
difficulties of human life,

00:28:41.480 --> 00:28:46.650
which turns into the root, the
wish, to eliminate them all.

00:28:46.650 --> 00:28:48.490
Do you see what
it's like to raise

00:28:48.490 --> 00:28:50.320
young children in this age?

00:28:50.320 --> 00:28:53.140
I mean, young children,
18 to 21-year-olds.

00:28:53.140 --> 00:28:54.975
That's our specialty.

00:28:54.975 --> 00:28:55.850
They're really great.

00:28:55.850 --> 00:28:56.725
They're almost human.

00:29:00.180 --> 00:29:02.070
And they grew up
in a world where

00:29:02.070 --> 00:29:05.370
they, at least in
the public discourse,

00:29:05.370 --> 00:29:09.030
they haven't been told
anything except that they can

00:29:09.030 --> 00:29:11.533
be whatever they want to be.

00:29:11.533 --> 00:29:13.200
And we don't get that
much, because this

00:29:13.200 --> 00:29:15.325
is a really weird place,
and it's hard to get into.

00:29:15.325 --> 00:29:17.590
And the kids you get in
here learned a lot about it.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:19.590
But once in a while they'll
say, well, you know,

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:22.333
I just want to become
whatever I can be.

00:29:22.333 --> 00:29:24.375
And I always say good,
you should be an aardvark.

00:29:29.450 --> 00:29:32.720
Or would you rather be a human?

00:29:32.720 --> 00:29:35.030
Because we know
what that's like.

00:29:35.030 --> 00:29:37.940
And we know what the
good ones are like.

00:29:37.940 --> 00:29:39.407
We take that away from them.

00:29:42.890 --> 00:29:46.360
This is a massive
and dangerous force.

00:29:46.360 --> 00:29:51.130
It may overcome us,
I said two days ago.

00:29:51.130 --> 00:29:56.160
I said, you know, this
is like the great crises.

00:29:56.160 --> 00:30:00.490
The Revolutionary War--
that was a fight about who

00:30:00.490 --> 00:30:03.010
is entitled to govern.

00:30:03.010 --> 00:30:06.190
Which is a fight always,
when it comes up,

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:08.610
about what is the human being.

00:30:08.610 --> 00:30:11.980
And the Civil War was
exactly the same thing.

00:30:11.980 --> 00:30:15.280
Isn't this just like it?

00:30:15.280 --> 00:30:18.550
Is it true that each of
us, in his common sense,

00:30:18.550 --> 00:30:20.500
has a right to govern
his own life, which

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:22.040
is what God gave him?

00:30:22.040 --> 00:30:24.550
According to a moral
and rational sense

00:30:24.550 --> 00:30:26.960
that only we among
creatures have.

00:30:26.960 --> 00:30:30.340
Or is it true that only people
who are experts and scientists

00:30:30.340 --> 00:30:31.450
can really pick our way?

00:30:34.440 --> 00:30:38.490
There's this guy
named Douglas Jay,

00:30:38.490 --> 00:30:41.250
and he made the mistake in
the first socialist government

00:30:41.250 --> 00:30:44.100
in Britain-- which beat
Winston Churchill--

00:30:44.100 --> 00:30:46.920
he made the mistake of saying,
within the House of Commons,

00:30:46.920 --> 00:30:51.930
in Churchill's hearing, he
said that mothers don't always

00:30:51.930 --> 00:30:54.840
know what is best
for their children.

00:30:54.840 --> 00:30:57.270
The gentlemen of
Whitehall know better.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:30:58.980
Whitehall is the
governing center.

00:30:58.980 --> 00:31:01.110
And you know, it was
just such a mistake

00:31:01.110 --> 00:31:03.150
to say that with
Winston Churchill,

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:04.590
because Winston
Churchill has made

00:31:04.590 --> 00:31:06.600
him famous to this
day for saying that.

00:31:09.630 --> 00:31:12.000
That's the spirit, though.

00:31:12.000 --> 00:31:14.100
And so, my last point is,
what do we do about it?

00:31:17.030 --> 00:31:19.540
First of all, it's
overwhelming, right?

00:31:19.540 --> 00:31:24.160
Everybody-- everybody
fancy, everybody elite.

00:31:24.160 --> 00:31:27.100
All these fancy graduate schools
and medical schools and law

00:31:27.100 --> 00:31:31.000
schools that our kids go to,
everybody at those schools

00:31:31.000 --> 00:31:32.175
disagrees with us.

00:31:32.175 --> 00:31:34.300
Sometimes, I think our
kids-- our kids are admitted

00:31:34.300 --> 00:31:36.320
because they're really smart.

00:31:36.320 --> 00:31:38.050
And you know,
they're impressive.

00:31:38.050 --> 00:31:43.500
But sometimes, I think they're
admitted because of curiosity.

00:31:43.500 --> 00:31:45.510
One young man who went
to Yale Law School

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:47.700
clerked for Clarence
Thomas later.

00:31:47.700 --> 00:31:51.950
Really, a super young
man, important man now.

00:31:51.950 --> 00:31:57.330
He-- it wasn't really
a slur, but at Yale, it

00:31:57.330 --> 00:32:03.090
was said to him,
you're just a white boy

00:32:03.090 --> 00:32:04.380
from Hillsdale College.

00:32:04.380 --> 00:32:07.300
You're out of your depth here.

00:32:07.300 --> 00:32:13.200
Now, I don't actually
mean to criticize the man.

00:32:13.200 --> 00:32:15.240
This young man later
found out that this guy

00:32:15.240 --> 00:32:16.830
was on the admissions
commission--

00:32:16.830 --> 00:32:18.750
committee that let him in.

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:19.890
Why did he let him in?

00:32:19.890 --> 00:32:21.570
He was curious.

00:32:21.570 --> 00:32:24.360
What kind of the people
are those people?

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:24.870
Right?

00:32:24.870 --> 00:32:26.970
And so now, we're
studied like specimens.

00:32:30.900 --> 00:32:35.920
So if that's overwhelming, how
can we do anything about it?

00:32:35.920 --> 00:32:38.650
Well, I'll just tell you
briefly what we're going to do.

00:32:38.650 --> 00:32:40.380
I mean, I'm granting, of course.

00:32:40.380 --> 00:32:42.640
Because, you know,
I want you know,

00:32:42.640 --> 00:32:45.180
marvelous things
have happened here.

00:32:45.180 --> 00:32:47.730
And we don't know how.

00:32:47.730 --> 00:32:51.250
We just got up every day and
thought we knew what to do.

00:32:51.250 --> 00:32:52.510
And we just got about it.

00:32:52.510 --> 00:32:56.650
And we got about it together
every day, day after day

00:32:56.650 --> 00:32:57.150
after day.

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:01.630
And, you know, that's a
pretty good way to work.

00:33:01.630 --> 00:33:04.350
But we're not deluded that
we're responsible for all this.

00:33:08.923 --> 00:33:10.340
But if great things
have happened,

00:33:10.340 --> 00:33:11.840
maybe you should
try to extend them.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:19.980
We're going to do two things.

00:33:19.980 --> 00:33:23.070
One is this little place,
with its graduate schools.

00:33:23.070 --> 00:33:24.850
You know, it's growing.

00:33:24.850 --> 00:33:28.480
We're kind of
frightened by that.

00:33:28.480 --> 00:33:32.620
It's going to be the most
intense atmosphere of learning

00:33:32.620 --> 00:33:34.930
on the face of the earth.

00:33:34.930 --> 00:33:36.660
It's already like that.

00:33:36.660 --> 00:33:40.240
You know, coming here, it's
like joining the Marines.

00:33:40.240 --> 00:33:47.284
Say goodbye to your 3.92 high
school grade point average.

00:33:47.284 --> 00:33:49.510
This is serious now.

00:33:49.510 --> 00:33:53.050
You know, I had a girl--

00:33:53.050 --> 00:33:54.610
Steve Smith is here tonight.

00:33:54.610 --> 00:33:56.140
He might be.

00:33:56.140 --> 00:33:58.280
Steve Smith and I have an honor.

00:33:58.280 --> 00:34:02.530
We once gave an A minus to a
girl who only got those two

00:34:02.530 --> 00:34:07.120
grades in her entire life, from
kindergarten, other than A's.

00:34:07.120 --> 00:34:09.639
And she cried in my office.

00:34:09.639 --> 00:34:11.030
And she said, what can I do?

00:34:11.030 --> 00:34:13.322
And I said, well, you should
probably try to do better.

00:34:16.820 --> 00:34:18.840
And this year-- you
know, she was sobbing.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:21.130
She was later our
valedictorian, by the way.

00:34:21.130 --> 00:34:22.810
And she was sobbing,
and she says,

00:34:22.810 --> 00:34:24.469
is there any chance
I can get an A?

00:34:24.469 --> 00:34:27.270
And I said, no.

00:34:27.270 --> 00:34:28.719
Get over it.

00:34:28.719 --> 00:34:31.770
That's the wrong thing
to think about, right?

00:34:31.770 --> 00:34:36.030
Well, the point is,
that's the ticket.

00:34:36.030 --> 00:34:38.699
You want to help them,
you want to love them,

00:34:38.699 --> 00:34:41.219
you want to nurture them.

00:34:41.219 --> 00:34:43.239
They got to work.

00:34:43.239 --> 00:34:44.909
They must not pretend
to achievements

00:34:44.909 --> 00:34:47.070
that they have not made.

00:34:47.070 --> 00:34:50.502
Now, when they fall,
you got to help them up.

00:34:50.502 --> 00:34:51.210
And they do fall.

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:56.100
They don't bring me in when
they fall because they're

00:34:56.100 --> 00:34:59.470
afraid I might kick them.

00:34:59.470 --> 00:35:02.030
But I won't, right?

00:35:02.030 --> 00:35:06.650
And this has got to be
tireless and relentless

00:35:06.650 --> 00:35:09.260
and no compromises.

00:35:09.260 --> 00:35:10.210
See.

00:35:10.210 --> 00:35:13.190
And I will tell you,
everybody I work with,

00:35:13.190 --> 00:35:16.010
and everybody on the board--
that Pat [INAUDIBLE] and Bill

00:35:16.010 --> 00:35:18.200
[INAUDIBLE] lead--

00:35:18.200 --> 00:35:20.322
that's what they think.

00:35:20.322 --> 00:35:21.530
They don't expect us to quit.

00:35:24.340 --> 00:35:25.310
Then what do we do?

00:35:25.310 --> 00:35:27.180
Because this can get
better than it is.

00:35:27.180 --> 00:35:28.330
And it's going to.

00:35:28.330 --> 00:35:32.100
I mean, God granting.

00:35:32.100 --> 00:35:35.430
That's what I call
the nuclear reactor.

00:35:35.430 --> 00:35:37.230
The general counsel
Bob Norton doesn't

00:35:37.230 --> 00:35:40.387
like me to say that it's
going to radiate the country,

00:35:40.387 --> 00:35:42.720
because he thinks there might
be some liability in that.

00:35:45.770 --> 00:35:47.750
I will say instead
that it's going

00:35:47.750 --> 00:35:50.768
to inspire something
in the country,

00:35:50.768 --> 00:35:52.060
and the means are laid out now.

00:35:55.170 --> 00:35:58.600
We have founded more than 20--

00:35:58.600 --> 00:36:01.112
oh, well, in Primus.

00:36:01.112 --> 00:36:04.410
It was 900,000 when I came here.

00:36:04.410 --> 00:36:07.690
It's soon to cross 5.5 million.

00:36:07.690 --> 00:36:10.994
[APPLAUSE]

00:36:14.298 --> 00:36:16.810
And let that stand
for the long list

00:36:16.810 --> 00:36:19.390
of incredibly stupid
things that we do here,

00:36:19.390 --> 00:36:21.680
because that thing
cost a fortune.

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:22.690
And you get it, right?

00:36:22.690 --> 00:36:25.180
We don't ask for
money in that day.

00:36:25.180 --> 00:36:26.410
And people send it anyway.

00:36:26.410 --> 00:36:28.280
I don't get it.

00:36:28.280 --> 00:36:30.430
It's really great.

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:35.110
And you know how many people we
talk to in a typical month now?

00:36:35.110 --> 00:36:39.890
15 to 20 million, counting
the radio, all that.

00:36:39.890 --> 00:36:41.940
And it means the college
has been famous--

00:36:41.940 --> 00:36:43.300
could become famous, right?

00:36:43.300 --> 00:36:46.060
Which means it's dangerous,
because bad people know

00:36:46.060 --> 00:36:48.070
who we are, too.

00:36:48.070 --> 00:36:48.910
Enemies, I mean.

00:36:52.210 --> 00:36:55.950
We're going to keep
that up and grow more.

00:36:55.950 --> 00:36:58.002
And then, we always
try-- and I, you know,

00:36:58.002 --> 00:36:59.460
I figured this out
a long time ago,

00:36:59.460 --> 00:37:01.410
because I have
this weird history.

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:03.760
I've worked in
nonprofits all my life.

00:37:03.760 --> 00:37:06.840
And I never intended
to, but I did.

00:37:06.840 --> 00:37:10.200
And I figured out
something about them.

00:37:10.200 --> 00:37:13.080
The things that they make
that's the most difficult thing

00:37:13.080 --> 00:37:15.360
that they make,
they don't actually

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:18.570
give to the people
who provide the money.

00:37:18.570 --> 00:37:19.450
It's a triangle.

00:37:19.450 --> 00:37:21.390
So General Motors that
makes cars, right?

00:37:21.390 --> 00:37:22.440
They sell it to a person.

00:37:22.440 --> 00:37:23.190
They use the car.

00:37:23.190 --> 00:37:24.960
And they give
General Motors money.

00:37:24.960 --> 00:37:28.020
That's a fairly
simple transaction.

00:37:28.020 --> 00:37:31.320
But you know, I'm looking
around for students here.

00:37:31.320 --> 00:37:33.790
There are some here somewhere.

00:37:33.790 --> 00:37:36.510
Maybe they've gone to bed.

00:37:36.510 --> 00:37:39.420
Probably not.

00:37:39.420 --> 00:37:42.900
You know, you don't know them.

00:37:42.900 --> 00:37:47.830
And we just lavish resources
on them and attention on them

00:37:47.830 --> 00:37:50.610
constantly.

00:37:50.610 --> 00:37:53.100
You don't get to see that.

00:37:53.100 --> 00:37:56.367
And yet you're paying for it.

00:37:56.367 --> 00:37:57.450
How do you make that work?

00:37:57.450 --> 00:38:00.090
Well, I decided a
long time ago, you

00:38:00.090 --> 00:38:05.110
have to do what you
do best for everybody.

00:38:05.110 --> 00:38:08.238
And so we have two things that
now reach very large audiences

00:38:08.238 --> 00:38:09.030
that are like that.

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:11.960
Well, forget the
events like this.

00:38:11.960 --> 00:38:14.400
Somewhere between 15,000
and 20,000 people a year

00:38:14.400 --> 00:38:16.980
come to Hillsdale
College events.

00:38:16.980 --> 00:38:18.900
And we never sell tickets.

00:38:18.900 --> 00:38:20.330
That, too, is really stupid.

00:38:24.990 --> 00:38:29.050
But we have these 20
plus charter schools now.

00:38:29.050 --> 00:38:31.670
And you know, that's
just a hell of a mess.

00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:34.190
And lord, if Waylen and
Bill [? Broadbeck ?]

00:38:34.190 --> 00:38:38.090
haven't hired my daughter
to try to fix it.

00:38:38.090 --> 00:38:39.620
And she will.

00:38:39.620 --> 00:38:42.770
And you know, it's great.

00:38:42.770 --> 00:38:44.720
And there are problems.

00:38:44.720 --> 00:38:46.632
And we're going to
sort the problems out.

00:38:46.632 --> 00:38:47.840
And they're going to become--

00:38:47.840 --> 00:38:51.350
I can tell you what
they're going to do.

00:38:51.350 --> 00:38:53.470
They're going to become
the greatest collaborative

00:38:53.470 --> 00:38:57.934
effort for school reform
in the United States.

00:38:57.934 --> 00:39:01.870
[APPLAUSE]

00:39:06.310 --> 00:39:08.240
They're just like
everything else.

00:39:08.240 --> 00:39:10.490
Some of the people who
run them are superb

00:39:10.490 --> 00:39:13.160
and some are not as superb.

00:39:13.160 --> 00:39:15.200
Some of the teachers
just knocked the cover

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:17.660
off the ball and others not.

00:39:17.660 --> 00:39:20.180
It's going to become a great
engine, so that the best

00:39:20.180 --> 00:39:22.770
ones help everybody else.

00:39:22.770 --> 00:39:27.150
And if you don't want to
get help, arrivederci.

00:39:30.030 --> 00:39:33.180
We have these online courses.

00:39:33.180 --> 00:39:37.290
And these two things are
two huge academic outreach

00:39:37.290 --> 00:39:38.520
programs.

00:39:38.520 --> 00:39:42.900
And they're both run
by children of mine.

00:39:42.900 --> 00:39:46.170
One of them's Katie,
and the other, Kyle,

00:39:46.170 --> 00:39:49.440
who, as I said the other
day, I've raised from pup.

00:39:49.440 --> 00:39:51.810
He's a student of
Hillsdale College.

00:39:51.810 --> 00:39:53.730
And you know, he's awesome.

00:39:53.730 --> 00:39:55.470
I mean, he's not here, right?

00:39:55.470 --> 00:40:00.030
And so, he's almost
finished with his PhD.

00:40:00.030 --> 00:40:01.180
He didn't want to get it.

00:40:01.180 --> 00:40:03.790
I made him do it.

00:40:03.790 --> 00:40:09.190
And what-- there are 2.2
million people taking notes,

00:40:09.190 --> 00:40:12.560
but they're getting
better all the time.

00:40:12.560 --> 00:40:16.990
They're going to be an
enormous engine for teaching

00:40:16.990 --> 00:40:20.050
to millions more than now.

00:40:20.050 --> 00:40:21.598
So finally, when
they're elaborated,

00:40:21.598 --> 00:40:23.140
there'll be two
things true about it.

00:40:23.140 --> 00:40:26.680
One is, if you would like
to get a liberal education

00:40:26.680 --> 00:40:28.810
in the second best way--

00:40:28.810 --> 00:40:31.540
the best way, in seminars
with people who really know

00:40:31.540 --> 00:40:35.260
and other people who are giving
their hearts to learning.

00:40:35.260 --> 00:40:37.360
The second best way--

00:40:37.360 --> 00:40:39.220
take these courses
and read these books

00:40:39.220 --> 00:40:41.140
and watch them over and over.

00:40:41.140 --> 00:40:43.630
And spit-- and make that
the activity of your life

00:40:43.630 --> 00:40:45.550
outside your work.

00:40:45.550 --> 00:40:48.290
You can get an A level
education that way.

00:40:48.290 --> 00:40:50.500
And here, it's free.

00:40:50.500 --> 00:40:53.270
And it's going to
cover everything.

00:40:53.270 --> 00:40:55.180
And there are millions doing it.

00:40:55.180 --> 00:40:58.420
And there will be, we
pray, a million more.

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:01.450
But then, take all that stuff
that we learned in the Barney

00:41:01.450 --> 00:41:05.460
project, in K through 12,
it's just like college.

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:07.390
There isn't any reason
not to have classrooms.

00:41:07.390 --> 00:41:08.710
We can afford it.

00:41:08.710 --> 00:41:10.660
In fact, we're wasting masses.

00:41:10.660 --> 00:41:13.540
Did you know that more
than half the employees

00:41:13.540 --> 00:41:16.600
of the public schools
are not schoolteachers?

00:41:16.600 --> 00:41:18.842
Which means half the
budget's shaved off

00:41:18.842 --> 00:41:20.910
and goes somewhere else.

00:41:20.910 --> 00:41:22.510
We can not just afford it.

00:41:22.510 --> 00:41:24.263
We can double afford it.

00:41:24.263 --> 00:41:26.680
But if you're not going to do
that because the schools are

00:41:26.680 --> 00:41:31.510
so bad, homeschoolers and
private schools and any school

00:41:31.510 --> 00:41:34.720
that want everything we get
great in the Barney project,

00:41:34.720 --> 00:41:37.510
we're going to digitize it
and make videos about it

00:41:37.510 --> 00:41:38.275
and put it online.

00:41:50.403 --> 00:41:51.820
We've come to think
of the college

00:41:51.820 --> 00:41:54.810
as the different kind of thing.

00:41:54.810 --> 00:41:57.120
It's a combination of two
things that have not been

00:41:57.120 --> 00:42:00.150
combined as far as we know.

00:42:00.150 --> 00:42:04.440
One of them is, it intends
to be the best college.

00:42:04.440 --> 00:42:07.000
It's not going to stop.

00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:09.440
And we get up in the
morning every day, saying,

00:42:09.440 --> 00:42:12.250
not good enough yet.

00:42:12.250 --> 00:42:16.030
It wouldn't get better if you
didn't do that, by the way.

00:42:16.030 --> 00:42:18.090
What's the second
thing is, it's going

00:42:18.090 --> 00:42:22.690
to be a means of
learning for millions

00:42:22.690 --> 00:42:25.740
and millions of people.

00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:28.970
And it's going to
radiate that everywhere.

00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:30.750
And I'll tell you
why it's important.

00:42:30.750 --> 00:42:33.240
There are two reasons.

00:42:33.240 --> 00:42:37.100
One of them is, that's
a very beautiful thing.

00:42:37.100 --> 00:42:40.880
And everybody should
see a beautiful thing,

00:42:40.880 --> 00:42:43.775
because then they will
know what God is like.

00:42:43.775 --> 00:42:47.840
Also, they will understand
their own freedom and why

00:42:47.840 --> 00:42:52.530
it is not to be interrupted.

00:42:52.530 --> 00:42:54.870
And the second
reason is because we

00:42:54.870 --> 00:42:58.950
live in an age of
confusion that has become

00:42:58.950 --> 00:43:02.700
dangerous, threatening tyranny.

00:43:02.700 --> 00:43:08.970
The only thing, ultimately, that
can dispel it is understanding.

00:43:08.970 --> 00:43:15.460
This college was born 175 years
ago to give that understanding.

00:43:15.460 --> 00:43:19.510
Not just here, even back
then, wherever anybody

00:43:19.510 --> 00:43:20.470
wanted to learn.

00:43:23.160 --> 00:43:30.200
You and God have given us
the means to make that big.

00:43:30.200 --> 00:43:32.160
And we're going
to save the world.

00:43:32.160 --> 00:43:33.270
Thank you.

